-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 5 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial (de minimis) threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Belmont Industries, the engagement team identifies a $200 misstatement in office supplies expense. Overall materiality for the audit is $75,000. Under AU-C 450, this misstatement is most appropriately classified as:', '["Clearly trivial and need not be accumulated on the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "A projected misstatement requiring extrapolation to the full population", "A factual misstatement that must be reported to those charged with governance", "An immaterial misstatement that should be included on the summary of uncorrected misstatements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, misstatements are clearly trivial when they are inconsequential individually and in the aggregate, using a threshold well below materiality (often 3–5% of materiality). At $200 against $75,000 materiality, this amount falls well below any reasonable clearly trivial threshold. Choice B is incorrect because projected misstatements result from extrapolating sample errors to a population, not from classifying individual known amounts.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of performance materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is performance materiality in the context of audit planning?', '["The maximum misstatement that would not affect financial statement users'' decisions", "The amount set below overall materiality to reduce the risk that aggregate uncorrected misstatements exceed materiality", "The threshold above which all misstatements must be corrected by management", "The amount used to determine whether a control deficiency is a material weakness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 320.09, performance materiality is set at an amount less than materiality for the financial statements as a whole to reduce to an appropriately low level the probability that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements exceeds materiality. Choice A is incorrect because it describes overall materiality, not performance materiality.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, what constitutes a control deficiency?', '["A material error discovered during substantive testing procedures", "A known misstatement that management has declined to correct", "A design or operation flaw where controls cannot prevent or detect misstatements timely", "An intentional manipulation of financial records by management"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a control deficiency exists when the design or operation of a control does not allow management or employees, in the normal course of performing their assigned functions, to prevent or detect and correct misstatements on a timely basis. Choice A is incorrect because a misstatement found during testing is an audit finding, not itself a control deficiency—though it may indicate one.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Fraud-related misstatement elevating deficiency classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Granite Peak Logistics, the auditor discovers that a warehouse manager fabricated $8,500 in expense reimbursements over six months by submitting receipts for personal purchases. Overall materiality is $200,000. Under AU-C 265, how should the auditor classify the related control deficiency?', '["As clearly trivial given the small dollar amount relative to materiality", "As a significant deficiency because the amount is below the material weakness threshold", "As a control deficiency requiring only verbal communication to management", "As at least a significant deficiency and potentially a material weakness, because fraud indicates a control breakdown regardless of the dollar amount"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, a control deficiency involving fraud—even if the amount is immaterial—is at least a significant deficiency and a strong indicator of a material weakness because it represents a breakdown in the control environment. The auditor must evaluate the nature of the deficiency, not merely its dollar impact. Choice A is incorrect because fraud-related deficiencies cannot be dismissed as trivial based solely on the monetary amount involved.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Iron curtain vs. rollover approach for evaluating misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Ridgeline Energy Corp. has carried forward $18,000 in uncorrected misstatements from the prior year. The current year audit identifies $22,000 in new misstatements, and overall materiality is $50,000. The auditor is evaluating the aggregate effect on the current year financial statements. Under AU-C 450, how does the iron curtain approach most likely affect this evaluation compared to the rollover approach?', '["The iron curtain approach results in a higher aggregate misstatement because it considers the cumulative balance sheet effect of all uncorrected misstatements, including prior-year carryovers", "The iron curtain approach results in a lower aggregate misstatement because it excludes prior-year misstatements that have already been evaluated", "Both approaches produce identical aggregate misstatement amounts when prior-year misstatements exist", "The rollover approach results in a higher aggregate misstatement because it incorporates the income statement effect of reversing prior-year items"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the iron curtain approach quantifies misstatements based on the cumulative balance sheet effect, capturing the total of $40,000 ($18,000 + $22,000) in uncorrected amounts. The rollover approach considers only the current-year income statement impact of $22,000. Choice B is incorrect because the iron curtain approach specifically includes, rather than excludes, the cumulative effect of prior-year uncorrected misstatements on the current balance sheet.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Definition of clearly trivial
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what does "clearly trivial" mean when evaluating identified misstatements?', '["The misstatement is below the performance materiality threshold established during planning", "The misstatement is inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate", "The misstatement was caused by an unintentional error rather than fraud", "The misstatement affects only the notes to the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, the auditor may designate an amount below which misstatements are clearly trivial, meaning they would be inconsequential whether taken individually or in the aggregate, and whether judged by any criteria of size, nature, or circumstances. Choice A is incorrect because clearly trivial is a threshold well below performance materiality, not equivalent to it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Evaluating deficiency with compensating IT control
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Software Solutions, the auditor finds that the accounts payable clerk can both create vendors and approve invoices without supervisory review. However, the IT department runs a monthly exception report identifying all payments to newly created vendors and management reviews it promptly. Under AU-C 265, how should the auditor evaluate this situation?', '["As a material weakness because the segregation of duties failure creates an unmitigated fraud risk", "As a significant deficiency because compensating controls can never fully address segregation of duties failures", "As a control deficiency whose severity is mitigated by the compensating detective control, reducing the likelihood of material misstatement", "As no deficiency because the compensating control eliminates the risk entirely"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, compensating controls are relevant to the auditor''s evaluation of whether a control deficiency, individually or in combination, constitutes a significant deficiency or material weakness. The monthly exception report mitigates, but does not eliminate, the risk from the segregation of duties failure. Choice A is incorrect because the compensating detective control reduces the reasonable possibility of a material misstatement, so classifying it as a material weakness overstates the severity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Purpose of the summary of uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cascade Valley Retail, the engagement team finds three individually immaterial misstatements: a $4,200 inventory overstatement, a $3,100 accounts receivable overstatement, and a $2,800 prepaid expense overstatement. The auditor records these on the summary of uncorrected misstatements. What is the primary purpose of this accumulation?', '["To determine whether each misstatement individually exceeds performance materiality", "To establish evidence that the auditor communicated all findings to management", "To calculate the projected population misstatement from sample errors", "To evaluate whether the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements is material to the financial statements as a whole"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor must accumulate misstatements identified during the audit, other than those that are clearly trivial, to evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in the aggregate. The combined $10,100 must be assessed against materiality. Choice C is incorrect because the summary of uncorrected misstatements includes known and projected amounts—its purpose is aggregation for comparison to materiality, not the projection calculation itself.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Management representation letter for uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At the conclusion of the audit of Thornfield Associates, the auditor presents management with a summary showing $31,000 in uncorrected misstatements against overall materiality of $90,000. Management declines to adjust the financial statements. Under AU-C 450, what specific representation must the auditor obtain from management regarding these uncorrected misstatements?', '["A written representation that management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "A verbal acknowledgment during the closing conference that management is aware of all identified misstatements", "A written representation that no additional misstatements exist beyond those identified by the auditor", "A signed schedule confirming the dollar amount of each uncorrected misstatement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.14, when the auditor has accumulated uncorrected misstatements, the auditor must request a written representation from management that it believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of such items should be included in or attached to the representation letter. Choice B is incorrect because a verbal acknowledgment does not satisfy the requirement—AU-C 450 specifically requires written representations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors changing misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Silverstone Pharmaceuticals, the auditor identifies a $15,000 misstatement in research and development expense—well below the $300,000 overall materiality. However, the misstatement results from management reclassifying certain costs to meet an analyst earnings forecast. How do the qualitative characteristics of this misstatement most likely affect the auditor''s evaluation?', '["They have no effect because the quantitative amount is clearly below materiality", "They elevate the significance of the misstatement because an intentional reclassification to meet external expectations may indicate management bias, requiring reconsideration of the risk of material misstatement due to fraud", "They reduce the significance because reclassification misstatements do not affect total net income", "They require the auditor to immediately issue an adverse opinion regardless of the dollar amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers qualitative factors—including whether a misstatement is intentional, designed to achieve a particular presentation, or masks a change in earnings trends—when evaluating materiality. A deliberate reclassification to meet earnings targets signals potential management bias under AU-C 240.A1. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 explicitly states that qualitative considerations can cause a quantitatively immaterial misstatement to be evaluated as material.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Three categories of misstatements in AU-C 450
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, which three categories are used to classify identified misstatements?', '["Intentional, unintentional, and estimated misstatements", "Quantitative, qualitative, and pervasive misstatements", "Factual, judgmental, and projected misstatements", "Material, immaterial, and clearly trivial misstatements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A3, identified misstatements are classified as factual misstatements (no doubt about the error), judgmental misstatements (arising from management''s judgments about estimates the auditor considers unreasonable), and projected misstatements (the auditor''s best estimate of misstatements in populations based on sample results). Choice A is incorrect because "intentional" and "unintentional" describe the cause of a misstatement, not the classification framework prescribed by AU-C 450.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Timing of written communication of deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, when must the auditor communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance?', '["Before the auditor begins substantive testing procedures", "Within 30 days of identifying the control deficiency", "Concurrently with the issuance of the management letter", "No later than 60 days following the report release date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This timeline ensures findings are documented promptly while allowing the auditor to complete the evaluation. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not impose a 30-day window from identification—the standard ties the deadline to the report release date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether to modify opinion based on scope limitation with unresolved misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cypress Bay Holdings, the auditor identifies $145,000 in projected misstatements from inventory sampling and $38,000 in factual misstatements in accounts payable. Overall materiality is $200,000 and performance materiality is $150,000. Management refuses to adjust any amounts and will not allow expanded testing of inventory. The auditor has been unable to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence about whether additional misstatements exist. Under AU-C 450 and AU-C 705, what is the most appropriate course of action?', '["Issue a qualified opinion due to the inability to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence, because the combined identified misstatements approach materiality and the scope limitation prevents resolution of the uncertainty", "Issue an unmodified opinion because the total identified misstatements of $183,000 do not exceed the $200,000 materiality threshold", "Issue an adverse opinion because the projected misstatements alone exceed performance materiality", "Withdraw from the engagement because management''s refusal to adjust constitutes a scope limitation that always precludes issuing any opinion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 705.08, when the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence and the possible effects could be material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate. The $183,000 in identified misstatements approaches materiality, and the scope restriction prevents determining whether further misstatements exist. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 450.11 requires considering the risk of undetected misstatements—the scope limitation means the aggregate could exceed materiality, making an unmodified opinion inappropriate.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate combined deficiencies in same process
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Whitehaven Medical Systems identifies three control deficiencies in the revenue cycle: (1) new patient billing codes are not verified against the approved code list before entry, (2) the system does not flag billing amounts exceeding 150% of the standard rate, and (3) monthly revenue reconciliations are performed but not reviewed by the controller. Each deficiency individually constitutes only a significant deficiency. Under AU-C 265, what should the auditor evaluate regarding the combined effect?', '["The deficiencies should be reported separately because each relates to a different phase of the revenue process", "Whether the combination of deficiencies in the same process constitutes a material weakness, because related deficiencies may together create a reasonable possibility of material misstatement", "Only the most severe individual deficiency should be elevated to a material weakness", "The deficiencies should be deferred to the next audit for further monitoring before reclassifying"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, the auditor must consider whether control deficiencies, individually or in combination, constitute significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. When multiple deficiencies affect the same transaction cycle, their combined effect may create a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement would not be prevented or detected. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 explicitly requires the auditor to evaluate the aggregate effect of related deficiencies rather than assessing each in isolation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of misstatement correction on audit procedures
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Briarwood Construction, the auditor identifies $62,000 in revenue recognition errors. Management agrees to correct all identified misstatements. After the adjustments are posted, the auditor notes that the misstatements all originated from the same automated billing routine. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s planned procedures?', '["No additional procedures are needed because management corrected all identified misstatements", "The auditor should expand the sample size to detect additional misstatements in the same population", "The auditor should reassess the risk of material misstatement related to the automated billing control and consider whether the control deficiency affects the nature and extent of further procedures", "The auditor should request that management engage an IT specialist to certify the billing system before the audit can continue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 315.31, when corrected misstatements reveal a systemic control weakness—such as a flawed automated routine—the auditor must reconsider the assessed risk of material misstatement and whether the planned nature, timing, and extent of further audit procedures remain appropriate. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450.08 requires the auditor to evaluate the implications of identified misstatements for the audit, even after management corrects them.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Design vs. operation deficiency distinction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the distinction between a control deficiency in design and a control deficiency in operation?', '["Design deficiencies involve IT controls while operation deficiencies involve manual controls", "Design deficiencies occur in startup companies while operation deficiencies occur in established entities", "Design deficiencies are always material weaknesses while operation deficiencies are always significant deficiencies", "A design deficiency means the control is missing or improperly designed, while an operation deficiency means a properly designed control does not function as intended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A5-A6, a deficiency in design exists when a necessary control is missing or not suitably designed to meet the control objective, even if operated as designed. A deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate effectively or is performed by someone lacking the necessary authority or qualifications. Choice A is incorrect because the design/operation distinction applies equally to both IT and manual controls.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of prior-period misstatement reversal
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Aspen Ridge Financial discovers that a $35,000 uncorrected misstatement from the prior year has reversed in the current period, reducing the current-year income statement effect to zero. However, last year''s balance sheet was overstated by $35,000. Overall materiality is $80,000 for both years. How does this reversal most likely affect the auditor''s current-year evaluation?', '["The reversal eliminates the misstatement entirely, so no current-year consideration is necessary", "Under the iron curtain approach, the auditor should still evaluate the cumulative balance sheet effect, which may differ from the income statement impact", "The auditor must require management to restate the prior-year financial statements before issuing the current-year opinion", "The reversal automatically converts the misstatement from factual to projected"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the iron curtain approach evaluates the cumulative effect on the ending balance sheet, while the rollover approach focuses on the current-year income statement effect. Even though the income statement impact reverses, the balance sheet may still carry effects of prior-year misstatements depending on the affected accounts. Choice A is incorrect because a misstatement''s reversal in the income statement does not eliminate the need to evaluate its cumulative balance sheet effect under the iron curtain methodology.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Evaluate response when management disputes deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Coast Enterprises, the auditor identifies a material weakness in the financial close process: journal entries over $50,000 are recorded without any independent review. Management disagrees with the classification, arguing that no misstatements resulted from the lack of review during the current year. Management refuses to acknowledge the material weakness in its response to the auditor''s written communication. Under AU-C 265 and professional standards, what should the auditor evaluate as the most appropriate response?', '["Accept management''s position because the absence of actual misstatements supports reclassifying the deficiency as a significant deficiency", "Withdraw from the engagement because management''s refusal to acknowledge a material weakness makes the audit impossible to complete", "Communicate the material weakness to those charged with governance regardless of management''s disagreement, because classification is based on the reasonable possibility of material misstatement, not on whether one actually occurred", "Defer the communication until the following year to determine if misstatements eventually result from the deficiency"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, the severity of a control deficiency depends on whether there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement could occur—not on whether one has occurred. The auditor''s judgment about deficiency classification is independent of management''s agreement. Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor must communicate material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance. Choice A is incorrect because the absence of actual misstatements does not reduce the reasonable possibility criterion used to evaluate deficiency severity.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Analyzing impact of IT general control deficiency on application controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Summit Trail Logistics, the auditor discovers that developers have unrestricted access to modify programs in the production accounting system without documented change management approval. Several automated application controls—including revenue cutoff calculations and inventory pricing—depend on this system. How does this IT general control deficiency most likely affect the auditor''s assessment of the related application controls?', '["It has no effect because application controls operate independently of the IT general control environment", "It only affects automated controls that were modified during the audit period", "It requires the auditor to re-perform all automated calculations rather than testing the application controls", "It undermines the reliability of the dependent automated application controls, requiring the auditor to assess whether those controls can still be relied upon"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 315.A72, IT general controls support the continued proper functioning of application controls. When change management controls are deficient, the auditor cannot assume automated application controls have operated consistently throughout the period. The auditor must evaluate whether the IT general control weakness creates a risk that application controls have been improperly altered. Choice A is incorrect because application controls are inherently dependent on IT general controls—a weakness in program change controls directly affects the reliability of automated processes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Communication recipients for material weaknesses
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, to whom must the auditor communicate all identified material weaknesses?', '["External regulatory agencies and the audit committee", "The entity''s internal audit department and management", "The entity''s legal counsel and the board of directors", "Those charged with governance and management"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.11-12, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses to those charged with governance in writing and must also communicate to management material weaknesses identified during the audit. This dual communication ensures both oversight bodies are informed. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not require the auditor to communicate control deficiencies directly to external regulatory agencies—that obligation may exist under separate regulatory requirements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Evaluating opinion when aggregate approaches materiality with qualitative concerns
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redstone Industrial Group, the auditor accumulates $85,000 in uncorrected misstatements against overall materiality of $100,000. Of these, $40,000 relates to management''s aggressive revenue recognition estimate that the auditor considers at the high end of the reasonable range, and $12,000 involves reclassification of an officer''s personal expense as a business cost. Management refuses to adjust. Under AU-C 450 and AU-C 700, how should the auditor evaluate the effect on the audit opinion?', '["Issue an unmodified opinion because the aggregate of $85,000 remains below the $100,000 materiality threshold", "Issue an adverse opinion because any amount of uncorrected fraud-related misstatements requires an adverse opinion", "Request management to adjust only the $12,000 fraud-related item and issue an unmodified opinion for the remainder", "Consider a modified opinion because the aggregate is close to materiality, qualitative factors including management bias and the nature of the personal expense elevate the risk, and undetected misstatements may push the total above materiality"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.11, when the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements approaches materiality, the auditor must consider the likelihood that undetected misstatements, combined with those accumulated, could exceed materiality. AU-C 450.A18 requires evaluating qualitative factors—the aggressive estimate suggests management bias, and the personal expense indicates potential fraud. Together, these factors may render the financial statements materially misstated. Choice A is incorrect because being below the quantitative threshold does not preclude a modified opinion when qualitative factors and the risk of undetected misstatements are considered.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Communication obligations for deficiencies below significant
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Lakeshore Community Credit Union, the auditor identifies a control deficiency where a teller occasionally forgets to initial deposit slips as verification. The deficiency does not rise to the level of a significant deficiency or material weakness. Under AU-C 265, what is the auditor''s communication obligation for this deficiency?', '["The auditor must include it in the written communication to those charged with governance alongside significant deficiencies", "The auditor has no obligation under AU-C 265 to communicate deficiencies less severe than a significant deficiency, though the auditor may choose to communicate them", "The auditor must verbally communicate it to management within 30 days of identification", "The auditor must document it in the engagement file and communicate it to the predecessor auditor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A16, the auditor is not required to communicate control deficiencies that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. However, the auditor may determine that it is appropriate to communicate other control deficiencies to management or those charged with governance. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265.11 limits the mandatory written communication to significant deficiencies and material weaknesses—lesser deficiencies are communicated at the auditor''s discretion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Projected misstatement calculation from sampling
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While testing accounts receivable at Foxglove Distribution, the auditor selects a statistical sample of 40 invoices from a population of 2,400 invoices totaling $1,800,000. The sample reveals misstatements totaling $6,000 from invoices with a combined book value of $30,000. Under AU-C 530, what is the projected misstatement for the population?', '["$6,000, because projected misstatements equal the total misstatements found in the sample", "$90,000, because the misstatement rate of 5% applied to the total invoice count yields 120 misstated invoices", "$360,000, because the 20% error rate from the sample is applied to the total population book value of $1,800,000", "$1,800,000, because any sampling error requires the auditor to assume the entire population is misstated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 530.A20, projected misstatements are calculated by extrapolating the error rate found in the sample to the entire population. The misstatement rate is $6,000 / $30,000 = 20%, and applying this rate to the $1,800,000 population yields $360,000 as the projected misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because the $6,000 represents only the known misstatement in the sample—failing to project it to the population would understate the likely total misstatement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Analyzing impact of misstatement trend indicating management bias
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Over three consecutive audits of Harborview Holdings, the auditor has identified uncorrected misstatements of $15,000, $18,000, and $22,000, all overstating revenue in the same product line through aggressive cutoff practices. Individually, each year''s misstatement is below the $80,000 materiality. What factor should the auditor most closely analyze when evaluating the current-year misstatement?', '["Whether the consistent pattern of overstatement in the same direction indicates management bias, which may affect the assessment of risks of material misstatement due to fraud", "Whether the cumulative three-year total of $55,000 should be divided by three to compare the average against current-year materiality", "Whether the prior-year misstatements have been corrected, since only current-year amounts are relevant to the current audit", "Whether the amounts should be aggregated across all three years and compared against the earliest year''s materiality level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers whether uncorrected misstatements might indicate a pattern suggesting management bias—particularly when misstatements consistently move in the same direction. A recurring overstatement of revenue through cutoff practices raises fraud risk considerations under AU-C 240.A1. Choice C is incorrect because AU-C 450.A16 explicitly requires the auditor to consider the effect of prior-period uncorrected misstatements on the current-year financial statements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Evaluating disclosure misstatement for related-party transaction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northwind Healthcare Systems, the auditor discovers that a $2.1 million related-party transaction with the CEO''s family member is not disclosed in the notes to the financial statements. Management argues the transaction was at arm''s length and disclosure is unnecessary. Overall materiality is $5 million. Under AU-C 450, how should the auditor treat this omission?', '["As clearly trivial because the transaction amount is below overall materiality", "As immaterial because the transaction was conducted at arm''s length pricing", "As a misstatement in the financial statement disclosures that must be evaluated for materiality considering both quantitative and qualitative factors", "As a scope limitation requiring the auditor to disclaim an opinion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, misstatements include inadequate or incomplete disclosures. AU-C 450.A18 requires both quantitative and qualitative evaluation—a related-party transaction with a senior executive''s family member carries significant qualitative weight regardless of whether it was at arm''s length. Omitting such disclosure could influence users'' economic decisions. Choice A is incorrect because clearly trivial relates to inconsequential amounts—related-party disclosures involving key management carry inherent qualitative significance under AU-C 550.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Subsequent discovery of control deficiency existing during audit period
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After fieldwork completion but before the report release date, the auditor of Cedarbrook Financial Partners learns that the entity''s payroll system was accessed by an unauthorized user on January 10, 2026, two weeks after the December 31, 2025, year-end. Investigation reveals the access was possible due to a control deficiency in user access management that existed throughout the audit period. Under AU-C 265, what is the auditor''s obligation?', '["No obligation because the unauthorized access occurred after year-end and does not affect the audited financial statements", "The auditor should evaluate the control deficiency and determine whether it represents a significant deficiency or material weakness requiring communication to those charged with governance", "The auditor must reopen fieldwork and retest all payroll transactions for the entire audit period", "The auditor should immediately notify law enforcement before communicating with the entity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.09, control deficiencies identified at any point during the audit must be evaluated for severity. Because the underlying access control weakness existed during the audit period, it is relevant to the auditor''s assessment even though the unauthorized access was discovered after year-end. The auditor must determine whether it rises to a significant deficiency or material weakness. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 requires evaluation of deficiencies that existed during the period under audit, regardless of when evidence of the deficiency surfaces.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluating response when prior-year material weakness remains unremediated
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In the prior year, the auditor of Copperfield Enterprises communicated a material weakness in the financial close process—specifically, that complex non-routine journal entries lacked independent review. During the current-year audit, the auditor discovers the same deficiency exists with no remediation. Management states they plan to address it next year. The prior-year communication met all AU-C 265 requirements. Under AU-C 265, what should the auditor evaluate when determining the current-year communication approach?', '["The auditor need not communicate the material weakness again because the prior-year communication remains in effect", "The auditor should downgrade the classification to a significant deficiency to reflect that no misstatements resulted from the deficiency", "The auditor should only reference the prior-year communication letter without issuing a new written communication", "The auditor must communicate the material weakness again in writing in the current year, because AU-C 265 requires communication of material weaknesses identified during each audit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A18, if a previously communicated significant deficiency or material weakness remains unremediated, the auditor must communicate it again in the current period. Each audit stands on its own, and the requirement for written communication applies to every engagement where the deficiency exists. The auditor may reference the prior communication but cannot substitute it. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not provide a carryover exemption—unremediated deficiencies must be recommunicated each year.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Definition of pervasive misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 705, what does it mean when misstatements are considered pervasive to the financial statements?', '["The misstatements are not confined to specific accounts and affect the financial statements as a whole, multiple elements, or a substantial proportion of the financial statements", "The misstatements have been identified in every subsidiary of a consolidated entity", "The misstatements exceed overall materiality by more than twice the established threshold", "The misstatements result from intentional fraud by senior management"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 705.05, misstatements are pervasive when they are not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items; when they represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements; or when they fundamentally affect users'' understanding of the financial statements as a whole. Pervasiveness describes the breadth of the effect, not its cause. Choice B is incorrect because pervasive does not require misstatements in every subsidiary—it refers to the scope of the effect on the financial statements, not the number of affected entities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — Analyzing interaction between misstatement accumulation and debt covenant compliance
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Stonebridge Manufacturing, the auditor accumulates $210,000 in uncorrected misstatements—all overstating assets—against overall materiality of $250,000. The entity''s debt covenant requires a minimum current ratio of 1.5:1, and the auditor calculates that correcting the misstatements would reduce the current ratio from 1.53:1 to 1.41:1, triggering a covenant violation. Management refuses to adjust. What interaction between AU-C 450 and AU-C 570 should the auditor most closely analyze?', '["Whether the uncorrected misstatements can be ignored because they do not individually exceed materiality", "Whether the covenant violation that would result from correction raises substantial doubt about going concern, which elevates the significance of the uncorrected misstatements beyond their quantitative amount", "Whether the lender must be notified directly by the auditor about the potential covenant breach", "Whether the misstatements should be reclassified from factual to projected to reduce their impact on the ratio"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, qualitative factors affecting the evaluation of misstatements include whether they affect compliance with debt covenants. When uncorrected misstatements mask a covenant breach, AU-C 570.A2 requires the auditor to consider whether conditions and events, including the masked breach, raise substantial doubt about going concern. The interplay between misstatement evaluation and going concern becomes critical. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 requires aggregate evaluation—the combined $210,000 approaches materiality and has covenant-triggering implications that cannot be ignored.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of accounting staff turnover on deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Ridgeview Community Hospital, the auditor learns that three of five accounting staff members resigned in September, and their replacements had no healthcare accounting experience. Since the turnover, the auditor has identified several errors in contractual adjustment calculations and revenue classification. How does this staffing change most likely affect the auditor''s evaluation of internal control deficiencies?', '["It indicates a potential deficiency in the entity''s risk assessment process, because the failure to ensure qualified personnel may have created conditions for multiple control failures", "It has no effect on control evaluation because staff turnover is an operational matter outside the scope of the audit", "It requires the auditor to pause the audit until the entity hires experienced replacements", "It converts all identified misstatements from factual to judgmental because the errors were caused by inexperience"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, the competence of personnel performing control activities is relevant to evaluating whether a control deficiency exists. When an entity fails to ensure adequate staffing with proper qualifications—a component of the risk assessment process under AU-C 315—multiple downstream control failures may follow. The auditor should evaluate whether the root cause represents a deficiency in the entity''s own risk assessment. Choice B is incorrect because the COSO framework and AU-C 315 recognize human resources policies as an integral part of the control environment.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
